```shiploop-state
{
  "evidence_refs": ["S1", "S2", "Exercise context: empty repository, no tools or files, unattended intake"],
  "headline": "Intake established a local Python CLI and test outcome, with recorded defaults for the unspecified CSV contract.",
  "outcome": "done",
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "K1.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "A person or script with sales CSV files uses the result through a local command-line entry point."
    },
    {
      "id": "K1.2",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Running the command on known sales records prints the correct total revenue, revenue for each region, and units for each product."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "In scope: a Python CLI that reads sales CSV files and prints the requested aggregates, plus tests. Out of scope: implementation during intake, deployment, data modification, and features beyond that request."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The repository is stated to be empty, so no existing application behavior is identified for preservation; discovery should verify any behavior that appears in the delivery checkout before changes."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume `python sales_summary.py FILE [FILE ...]`; each UTF-8 CSV has a header containing `region`, `product`, `units`, and `revenue`. Treat each row as one record, sum signed integer units and finite decimal revenue, and combine all input files. Print `Total revenue: VALUE`, then `Revenue per region:` and sorted `  REGION: VALUE` lines, then `Units per product:` and sorted `  PRODUCT: VALUE` lines. Use exact decimal arithmetic and plain decimal output without imposed rounding. Assume tests reside in `test_sales_summary.py`. Discovery must establish the actual record schema and expected output contract before these defaults are relied upon."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume exit status 0 on success and 1 for missing arguments, unreadable files, malformed CSV, or invalid records; errors go to stderr without a traceback, and no summary is printed on failure. Discovery should check for an applicable existing CLI error convention."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Aggregate every record from every supplied file, including duplicates; allow signed amounts and units so returns can be represented. A header-only input contributes zero and produces empty grouped sections. Processing ends after printing the summary or reporting an error."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume missing required headers, blank region or product, non-integer units, non-finite or invalid decimal revenue, malformed CSV, and invalid UTF-8 are errors; extra columns are ignored. Discovery must establish whether real sales records require different validation."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The invoking process owns in-memory aggregates for one command invocation; they end when the process exits. Input files remain owned by their existing owners and are read only."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The invoker and anyone with access to its stdout or stderr can see output; the tool provides no additional access control. Concurrent changes to input files have no guaranteed snapshot semantics; a restart rereads the files and recomputes the summary."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume Python 3.10 or later; discovery should establish the runtime available in the delivery environment before implementation."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume Python standard-library dependencies only; no third-party package is needed for the recorded contract."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.3",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume file paths are supplied as command arguments, with no ports, environment variables, or configuration files required."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Keep CSV parsing and validation, aggregation, output formatting, and CLI error handling separable so each can be changed and checked independently."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume the standard-library unittest framework and command `python -m unittest`."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Run the actual CLI against temporary CSV fixtures and check stdout and exit status for all three aggregates, multiple files, and empty data; check stderr and exit status for representative invalid input. Use focused tests for decimal aggregation and validation where useful."
    },
    {
      "id": "K9.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Deliver runnable source and tests in the run's source checkout for local command-line use; execution needs Python and read access to the supplied CSV files. No deployed target is named."
    },
    {
      "id": "K10.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "No numeric performance, accessibility, or interface-polish bounds are specified. Assume ordinary local CLI operation, exact decimal revenue arithmetic, and no network access or modification of input files."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The most likely rejection is a mismatch between the assumed CSV columns or revenue meaning and the actual sales records, or an incorrect aggregation."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Discovery of an authoritative sample or schema would expose a contract mismatch early; a small fixture with independently calculated totals would expose aggregation errors before delivery."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "S1: Outcome—produce a local Python CLI whose printed totals are usable by the invoker at the command line after the source is returned; the isolated run's source checkout remains at its snapshot until final return, and no deployed target or release is requested. Facts—sales CSV input and the three named aggregates are requested, the repository is stated to be empty, and this unattended intake has no tools or files. Assumptions—the recorded CLI, CSV, validation, output, runtime, and error defaults in the answers apply until evidence supersedes them; the process has only invocation-local state, identifies no users, shares no state across people, devices, or sessions, and provides no hidden data or access controls beyond the execution environment. Open questions—discovery must first seek an authoritative sales schema or representative records to establish column names, revenue meaning, valid values, and return handling; this blocks confidence in the input contract and totals. Discovery must establish any required output and error conventions and the available Python runtime from the delivery environment before implementation relies on those defaults; these block compatibility decisions. Discovery must establish whether the runtime supplies identity, shared or persistent state, or privacy requirements before any such behavior is designed; absent evidence, the recorded stateless local-process default rules those features out. Source hints are leads only: relevant team or workspace, communication channels, internal sites and documentation, private repositories, data owners, and ambiguous terms such as 'sales records', 'revenue', and 'region'; none is identified or verified in the request. Any sensitive locator belongs only in an authorized private store, never in committed KNOWLEDGE/. S2: Outcome—deliver tests with the source, including checks at the actual CLI boundary; the recorded unittest command and fixture approach are defaults, and discovery should establish any delivery-check conventions before they are relied upon."
}
```
